## A2 --> A3[CRDT Engine]

The write‑front‑end has now stamped every client mutation with a Hybrid Logical Clock (HLC) and handed the resulting command to the Axon command bus. The next hop – the **CRDT Engine** – is the heart of causal convergence. Its job is to transform a raw `KvCommand` into a *conflict‑free replicated data type* (CRDT) operation, apply the operation to the in‑memory replica, and emit a durable `CrdtEvent` that will be replayed on every geo‑node. Because the engine lives inside the same Spring‑Boot process as the HLC generator, it can guarantee that the HLC timestamp travels unchanged through the CRDT merge logic, preserving causal ordering across regions.

### Low‑level design (LLD)

The engine is modelled as a small OOP hierarchy that isolates type‑specific merge rules while exposing a uniform command‑handler interface.

    public interface Crdt {
        CrdtType type();
        void apply(CrdtCommand cmd);
        Crdt merge(Crdt other);
    }

    public enum CrdtType { GCOUNTER, PN_COUNTER, OR_SET }

    public abstract class AbstractCrdt implements Crdt {
        protected final String id;
        protected final long hlc;   // HLC timestamp of the last mutation
        protected AbstractCrdt(String id, long hlc) { this.id=id; this.hlc=hlc; }
        public String getId() { return id; }
        public long getHlc() { return hlc; }
    }

    public final class GCounter extends AbstractCrdt {
        private long value;
        public GCounter(String id, long hlc) { super(id, hlc); }
        @Override public CrdtType type() { return CrdtType.GCOUNTER; }
        @Override public void apply(CrdtCommand cmd) {
            this.value += cmd.getDelta();
            // hlc is monotonic, so we simply adopt the incoming timestamp
        }
        @Override public Crdt merge(Crdt other) {
            GCounter o = (GCounter) other;
            GCounter merged = new GCounter(id, Math.max(this.hlc, o.hlc));
            merged.value = Math.max(this.value, o.value);
            return merged;
        }
        public long get() { return value; }
    }

    // PN‑Counter and OR‑Set follow the same pattern, each overriding merge().

The Spring REST façade delegates to the Axon command bus, which in turn invokes the engine:

