## C1[Write Frontend] --> C2[HLC Generator]

When a client submits a write operation, the request first lands on the **Write Frontend** (C1). The frontend’s responsibility is to validate the payload, enrich it with a causal timestamp, and hand it off to the **Hybrid Logical Clock (HLC) Generator** (C2). The HLC generator is a thin, stateless component that guarantees monotonically increasing logical time while preserving real‑world causality. In a multi‑master geo‑replicated environment, the HLC timestamp is the sole source of ordering for all subsequent conflict‑resolution logic.

### 1. Validation & Payload Normalization

The REST controller receives a JSON body that describes the key, value, and optional metadata. Validation is performed using Spring’s `@Valid` mechanism and a custom `@HLCAware` annotation that ensures the request contains the necessary fields. After validation, the controller constructs a `WriteCommand` domain object. This command encapsulates the key, value, and a freshly generated HLC timestamp.

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/kv")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class WriteController {

    private final HlcService hlcService;
    private final CommandGateway commandGateway;
    private final CircuitBreakerRegistry cbRegistry;

    @PostMapping
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "writeCommand", fallbackMethod = "fallback")
    public Mono<ResponseEntity<Void>> write(@Valid @RequestBody WriteRequest request) {
        HlcTimestamp ts = hlcService.now();
        WriteCommand cmd = new WriteCommand(request.getKey(), request.getValue(), ts);
        return commandGateway.send(cmd)
                .thenReturn(ResponseEntity.accepted().build());
    }

    private Mono<ResponseEntity<Void>> fallback(Throwable t, WriteRequest request) {
        // Graceful degradation: log and return 503
        return Mono.just(ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE).build());
    }
}
```

### 2. HLC Generation Logic

The `HlcService` is a singleton bean that maintains the local physical clock and a logical counter. The HLC algorithm is implemented in pure Java, avoiding any external dependencies. The service exposes a `now()` method that returns a new `HlcTimestamp` instance. The timestamp is immutable and serializable so it can be embedded in the command and later persisted in the event store.

```java
@Service
public class HlcService {

    private final AtomicLong logical = new AtomicLong(0);
    private volatile long physical = System.currentTimeMillis();

    public synchronized HlcTimestamp now() {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
        if (now > physical) {
            physical = now;
            logical.set(0);
        } else {
            logical.incrementAndGet();
        }
        return new HlcTimestamp(physical, logical.get());
    }
}
```

### 3. Domain Model – Commands & Events

The write operation is modeled as a command that is processed by Axon’s command bus. The command carries the HLC timestamp, which is later used by the event handler to produce a causal event. The event includes the same timestamp, ensuring that downstream replicas can order events deterministically.

```java
public interface WriteCommand {
    String getKey();
    String getValue();
    HlcTimestamp getTimestamp();
}

public record WriteCommandImpl(String key, String value, HlcTimestamp timestamp) implements WriteCommand {}

public interface WriteEvent {
    String getKey();
    String getValue();
    HlcTimestamp getTimestamp();
}

public record WriteEventImpl(String key, String value, HlcTimestamp timestamp) implements WriteEvent {}
```

### 4. Axon Command Handling

The command handler receives the `WriteCommand`, creates a `WriteEvent`, and publishes it to the event store. Axon’s event sourcing guarantees that the event is persisted before the handler completes. The event is then forwarded to Kafka via a Spring Kafka producer, wrapped in a Resilience4j circuit breaker to guard against transient broker failures.

```java
@Component
public class WriteCommandHandler {

    private final EventBus eventBus;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, WriteEvent> kafkaTemplate;
    private final CircuitBreakerRegistry cbRegistry;

    @CommandHandler
    public void handle(WriteCommand cmd) {
        WriteEvent evt = new WriteEventImpl(cmd.getKey(), cmd.getValue(), cmd.getTimestamp());
        eventBus.publish(evt);
        CircuitBreaker cb = cbRegistry.circuitBreaker("kafkaPublish");
        cb.executeRunnable(() -> kafkaTemplate.send("kv-writes", evt.getKey(), evt));
    }
}
```

