## C3 --> C4[Local Replica Store]

The local replica store is the heart of a geo‑replicated KV system that guarantees causal consistency without a global clock. It stores a **causal CRDT map** whose entries are annotated with Hybrid Logical Clock (HLC) timestamps. The store is a pure Java component that can be embedded in any Spring‑Boot application, exposing a lightweight REST API and participating in a Kafka‑based replication pipeline.  

### Domain Model (LLD)

```java
// Core abstraction for a CRDT entry
public interface CrdtEntry<V> {
    V getValue();
    HlcTimestamp getTimestamp();
}

// Abstract base that implements common merge logic
public abstract class AbstractCrdtEntry<V> implements CrdtEntry<V> {
    protected final V value;
    protected final HlcTimestamp ts;

    protected AbstractCrdtEntry(V value, HlcTimestamp ts) {
        this.value = value;
        this.ts = ts;
    }

    @Override public V getValue() { return value; }
    @Override public HlcTimestamp getTimestamp() { return ts; }
}

// Concrete entry types
public class GCounterEntry extends AbstractCrdtEntry<Long> {
    public GCounterEntry(Long value, HlcTimestamp ts) { super(value, ts); }
}

public class LWWRegisterEntry<V> extends AbstractCrdtEntry<V> {
    public LWWRegisterEntry(V value, HlcTimestamp ts) { super(value, ts); }
}

// Repository interface
public interface CrdtStore {
    <V> void put(String key, CrdtEntry<V> entry);
    <V> CrdtEntry<V> get(String key);
    void delete(String key, HlcTimestamp ts);
    Set<String> keys();
}
```

The `CrdtStore` is implemented as an in‑memory `ConcurrentHashMap` for demonstration, but any durable backing (e.g., RocksDB, LevelDB) can be plugged in by providing another implementation of the interface.

### REST Endpoint (Spring)

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/kv")
public class KvController {

    private final CrdtStore store;
    private final HlcClock clock;

    public KvController(CrdtStore store, HlcClock clock) {
        this.store = store;
        this.clock = clock;
    }

    @PostMapping("/{key}")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> put(@PathVariable String key,
                                    @RequestBody String value) {
        HlcTimestamp ts = clock.now();
        CrdtEntry<String> entry = new LWWRegisterEntry<>(value, ts);
        store.put(key, entry);
        return ResponseEntity.accepted().build();
    }

    @GetMapping("/{key}")
    public ResponseEntity<String> get(@PathVariable String key) {
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        CrdtEntry<String> entry = (CrdtEntry<String>) store.get(key);
        if (entry == null) return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        return ResponseEntity.ok(entry.getValue());
    }
}
```

The controller delegates to the store and uses the local HLC instance to stamp writes. Reads are served from the local state, guaranteeing that all causal dependencies are already satisfied.

