## A1[Write Frontend] --> A2[HLC Generator]

When a client submits a write through the REST API, the request first lands on the **Write Frontend** (`A1`). The frontend is a thin Spring MVC controller whose sole responsibility is to translate the HTTP payload into a domain command and hand it off to the **Hybrid Logical Clock (HLC) Generator** (`A2`). The HLC generator is a stateless component that stamps every command with a causally‑consistent timestamp before it is persisted or replicated.  
Below is a step‑by‑step walk‑through of the flow, the domain model, and the supporting infrastructure.

### 1. Domain Model (LLD)

```java
interface KeyValueCommand {
    String getKey();
    Object getValue();
    HLC getTimestamp();
}

abstract class AbstractCommand implements KeyValueCommand {
    private final String key;
    private final Object value;
    private final HLC timestamp;
    protected AbstractCommand(String key, Object value, HLC timestamp) {
        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;
        this.timestamp = timestamp;
    }
    public String getKey() { return key; }
    public Object getValue() { return value; }
    public HLC getTimestamp() { return timestamp; }
}

class PutCommand extends AbstractCommand {
    PutCommand(String key, Object value, HLC timestamp) {
        super(key, value, timestamp);
    }
}

class DeleteCommand extends AbstractCommand {
    DeleteCommand(String key, Object value, HLC timestamp) {
        super(key, value, timestamp);
    }
}

enum CommandType { PUT, DELETE }

class HLC {
    private final long logical;
    private final long physical;
    private final int counter;
    HLC(long logical, long physical, int counter) {
        this.logical = logical;
        this.physical = physical;
        this.counter = counter;
    }
    // merge, increment, and serialization helpers omitted for brevity
}
```

### 2. REST Endpoint

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/kv")
class KvController {
    private final KvService kvService;
    KvController(KvService kvService) { this.kvService = kvService; }

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Void> write(@RequestBody PutRequest req) {
        HLC timestamp = HlcGenerator.next();   // A2
        PutCommand cmd = new PutCommand(req.getKey(), req.getValue(), timestamp);
        kvService.apply(cmd);
        return ResponseEntity.ok().build();
    }
}
```

### 3. Service Layer (Architecture Pattern)

```java
@Service
class KvService {
    private final CommandGateway commandGateway;
    KvService(CommandGateway commandGateway) { this.commandGateway = commandGateway; }

    @CircuitBreaker(name="kv-service", fallbackMethod="fallback")
    @Retry(name="kv-service")
    public void apply(AbstractCommand cmd) {
        commandGateway.sendAndWait(cmd);   // Axon command bus
    }

    private void fallback(AbstractCommand cmd, Throwable t) {
        // graceful degradation: log and retry asynchronously
    }
}
```

The `CommandGateway` is backed by Spring Kafka; every command is serialized and published to the `kv-commands` topic, ensuring that all replicas receive the same HLC‑tagged payload.

### 4. Data‑Structure Algorithms (DSA)

| Problem | Approach | Complexity |
|---------|----------|------------|
| Merge two sorted lists | Two‑pointer scan | O(n+m) |
| Median of two sorted arrays | Binary search partition | O(log min(n,m)) |
| Longest increasing subsequence | Patience sorting + binary search | O(n log n) |

```java
// Merge two sorted lists
List<Integer> merge(List<Integer> a, List<Integer> b) {
    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
    int i=0,j=0;
    while(i<a.size() && j<b.size()){
        if(a.get(i)<=b.get(j)){ res.add(a.get(i++)); }
        else{ res.add(b.get(j++)); }
    }
    while(i<a.size()){ res.add(a.get(i++)); }
    while(j<b.size()){ res.add(b.get(j++)); }
    return res;
}

// Median of two sorted arrays
double findMedianSortedArrays(int[] A, int[] B) {
    if(A.length > B.length) return findMedianSortedArrays(B, A);
    int m=A.length, n=B.length;
    int imin=0, imax=m, halfLen=(m+n+1)/2;
    while(imin<=imax){
        int i=(imin+imax)/2;
        int j=halfLen-i;
        if(i< m && B[j-1] > A[i]) imin=i+1;
        else if(i>0 && A[i-1] > B[j]) imax=i-1;
        else {
            int maxLeft = 0;
            if(i==0) maxLeft=B[j-1];
            else if(j==0) maxLeft=A[i-1];
            else maxLeft=Math.max(A[i-1], B[j-1]);

            if((m+n)%2==1) return maxLeft;
            int minRight = 0;
            if(i==m) minRight=B[j];
            else if(j==n) minRight=A[i];
            else minRight=Math.min(A[i], B[j]);

            return (maxLeft + minRight)/2.0;
        }
    }
    throw new IllegalArgumentException();
}

// Longest increasing subsequence
int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
    int[] tails = new int[nums.length];
    int size = 0;
    for(int x: nums){
        int i=0, j=size;
        while(i<j){
            int m=(i+j)/2;
            if(tails[m] < x) i=m+1; else j=m;
        }
        tails[i]=x;
        if(i==size) size++;
    }
    return size;
}
```

