### 7. HLC vs. Other Logical Clocks

| Clock Type | Monotonicity | Real‑World Awareness | Storage Overhead | Use‑Case Suitability |
|------------|--------------|----------------------|------------------|----------------------|
| Lamport     | Yes          | No                   | 1 long           | Simple ordering      |
| Vector Clock| Yes          | No                   | N longs (N = nodes) | Fine‑grained causality |
| Hybrid Logical Clock | Yes | Yes (via physical component) | 2 longs | Geo‑replicated writes with minimal coordination |

The HLC’s two‑component structure (physical + logical) gives it a distinct advantage in geo‑distributed systems: it preserves real‑time ordering across regions while still allowing local increments without network round‑trips. This makes it the ideal choice for the write path in a multi‑master KV store where latency and causal consistency are paramount.

## C2 --> C3[CRDT Engine]

The CRDT engine sits between the HLC‑tagged write‑path (C2) and the storage layer (C4). Its responsibility is to merge concurrent updates locally and propagate a *commutative, associative, idempotent* state that can be safely replicated across all replicas.  
Below is a compact, yet complete, Java OOP domain model that captures the essential abstractions, followed by a Spring REST endpoint that exposes CRUD operations. The engine is wired to Spring Kafka for asynchronous dissemination and protected by Resilience4j’s circuit breaker to tolerate transient broker outages.

### Domain Model (LLD)

```java
// 1. CRDT abstraction
public interface CRDT<T> {
    T getValue();
    void merge(CRDT<T> other);
}

// 2. HLC wrapper
public final class HLC {
    private final long physical;   // nanoseconds
    private final long logical;    // counter
    // constructor, getters, compareTo omitted for brevity
}

// 3. Base CRDT with HLC metadata
public abstract class AbstractCRDT<T> implements CRDT<T> {
    protected final HLC timestamp;
    protected AbstractCRDT(HLC ts) { this.timestamp = ts; }
    public HLC getTimestamp() { return timestamp; }
}

// 4. Concrete CRDTs
public class GCounter extends AbstractCRDT<Long> {
    private long count;
    public GCounter(HLC ts, long init) { super(ts); this.count = init; }
    public Long getValue() { return count; }
    public void merge(CRDT<Long> other) {
        GCounter o = (GCounter) other;
        this.count = Math.max(this.count, o.count);
    }
}

public class LWWRegister<T> extends AbstractCRDT<T> {
    private T value;
    public LWWRegister(HLC ts, T val) { super(ts); this.value = val; }
    public T getValue() { return value; }
    public void merge(CRDT<T> other) {
        LWWRegister<T> o = (LWWRegister<T>) other;
        if (o.getTimestamp().compareTo(this.timestamp) > 0) {
            this.value = o.value;
            this.timestamp = o.timestamp;
        }
    }
}

// 5. Enum for type dispatch
public enum CRDTType { GCOUNTER, LWWREGISTER }
```

