## Op) --> C3

The operational core of a causal KV store hinges on three tightly coupled concerns: **(1) a rich domain model that can express versioned keys, conflict‑free merge semantics, and replica identity; (2) a resilient command‑processing pipeline that guarantees at‑least‑once delivery across multi‑master sites; and (3) a set of auxiliary utilities (DSA helpers, AI‑assisted query enrichment, and performance‑tuned before/after refactors) that keep the system both developer‑friendly and production‑ready. The following subsections walk through each concern step‑by‑step, showing how the pieces fit together without re‑introducing concepts already covered in the causal‑ordering primer.

### 1. Low‑Level Domain Model (Java OOP)

The model is deliberately split into interfaces, an abstract base, concrete implementations, and a small enum that captures the merge strategy. All classes live in the `com.example.causalkv.domain` package and are deliberately free of persistence annotations – the store layer is injected later by Axon‑Kafka event handlers.

    package com.example.causalkv.domain;

    /** Marker for any versioned entity. */
    public interface Versioned {
        HybridLogicalTimestamp getTimestamp();
    }

    /** Enum describing how two divergent values are reconciled. */
    public enum MergePolicy {
        LWW,            // Last‑Write‑Wins based on HLC
        CRDT_SET,      // Set‑based CRDT merge
        CRDT_GCOUNTER  // Grow‑only counter merge
    }

    /** Abstract skeleton that supplies common fields. */
    public abstract class AbstractEntry implements Versioned {
        protected final String key;
        protected final HybridLogicalTimestamp timestamp;
        protected final MergePolicy policy;

        protected AbstractEntry(String key, HybridLogicalTimestamp ts, MergePolicy policy) {
            this.key = key;
            this.timestamp = ts;
            this.policy = policy;
        }

        public String getKey() { return key; }
        public HybridLogicalTimestamp getTimestamp() { return timestamp; }
        public MergePolicy getPolicy() { return policy; }

        /** Sub‑classes must implement a merge that respects the policy. */
        public abstract AbstractEntry merge(AbstractEntry other);
    }

    /** Concrete entry for simple scalar values. */
    public final class ScalarEntry extends AbstractEntry {
        private final String value;

        public ScalarEntry(String key, HybridLogicalTimestamp ts, MergePolicy policy, String value) {
            super(key, ts, policy);
            this.value = value;
        }

        public String getValue() { return value; }

        @Override
        public ScalarEntry merge(AbstractEntry other) {
            if (!(other instanceof ScalarEntry o)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Incompatible merge");
            // LWW merge – pick the later timestamp.
            return (this.timestamp.isAfter(o.timestamp)) ? this : o;
        }
    }

    /** Concrete entry for grow‑only counters. */
    public final class GCounterEntry extends AbstractEntry {
        private final long count;

        public GCounterEntry(String key, HybridLogicalTimestamp ts, long count) {
            super(key, ts, MergePolicy.CRDT_GCOUNTER);
            this.count = count;
        }

        public long getCount() { return count; }

        @Override
        public GCounterEntry merge(AbstractEntry other) {
            if (!(other instanceof GCounterEntry o)) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Incompatible merge");
            // CRDT‑GCounter merge – take the max.
            long merged = Math.max(this.count, o.count);
            HybridLogicalTimestamp mergedTs = this.timestamp.isAfter(o.timestamp) ? this.timestamp : o.timestamp;
            return new GCounterEntry(this.key, mergedTs, merged);
        }
    }

    /** Simple HLC implementation used throughout the model. */
    public final class HybridLogicalTimestamp implements Comparable<HybridLogicalTimestamp> {
        private final long physical;   // NTP‑synchronised wall‑clock
        private final int logical;     // Monotonically increasing per node

        public HybridLogicalTimestamp(long physical, int logical) {
            this.physical = physical;
            this.logical = logical;
        }

        public static HybridLogicalTimestamp now() {
            return new HybridLogicalTimestamp(System.currentTimeMillis(), 0);
        }

        public HybridLogicalTimestamp increment() {
            return new HybridLogicalTimestamp(physical, logical + 1);
        }

        @Override
        public int compareTo(HybridLogicalTimestamp o) {
            int cmp = Long.compare(this.physical, o.physical);
            return (cmp != 0) ? cmp : Integer.compare(this.logical, o.logical);
        }

        public boolean isAfter(HybridLogicalTimestamp o) {
            return this.compareTo(o) > 0;
        }

        @Override public String toString() {
            return physical + "-" + logical;
        }
    }

### 2. Spring REST Endpoint + Axon Command Gateway

The controller is a thin façade that translates HTTP payloads into Axon commands. Resilience4j’s `Retry` and `CircuitBreaker` wrappers protect the gateway against transient Kafka outages.

    package com.example.causalkv.api;

    import org.axonframework.commandhandling.gateway.CommandGateway;
    import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
    import io.github.resilience4j.retry.annotation.Retry;
    import io.github.resilience4j.circuitbreaker.annotation.CircuitBreaker;

    @RestController
    @RequestMapping("/kv")
    public class KvController {

        private final CommandGateway gateway;

        public KvController(CommandGateway gateway) {
            this.gateway = gateway;
        }

        @PostMapping("/{key}")
        @Retry(name = "kvRetry")
        @CircuitBreaker(name = "kvCircuit")
        public void put(@PathVariable String key,
                        @RequestBody String value,
